      "writtenOn": "2013-10-30T11:50:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Seeing all these base64_foo functions makes me wonder if this would be better as a utility class, which you initialize with text.\n\nI mean, as written this is going to lead to horrible base64-naming-overload and needlessly long lines:\n\nfrom keystone.common import base64utils\n\nbase64utils.base64url_to_base64(\"blah\")",
      "revId": "1f872abb80c262e10730d86d1ef068bda0dea989",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAATn//lSw\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/common/base64utils.py",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 242,
      "author": {
        "id": 8116
      },
      "writtenOn": "2013-11-07T00:26:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I understand your suggestion for encapsulating this in a class, in fact I did consider that. In actual practice using these functions (including those in pemutils) it seemed easier to have a toolbox of functions that could be applied to any Python basestring rather than having to serialize into and out of a class object as different \"transforms\" are applied.\n\nI have sympathy for your observation concerning long names, I suppose the \u0027base64\u0027 components of the identifiers could be shortened to \u0027b64\u0027. I\u0027d be happy to make that change if you like.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAATn//sME\u003d",
      "revId": "1f872abb80c262e10730d86d1ef068bda0dea989",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
